{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to expire on Sunday, as a new law will make it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs once again. The new law, HB4002, will give individuals caught with illicit substances the option to either face charges or participate in a treatment program. Those who choose treatment will be required to complete a behavioral health program and participate in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\nPersonal-use possession of hard drugs will now be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas such as parks.\n\nThe law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, opponents argue that this may lead to a confusing and inequitable patchwork of policy.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day explained that the criteria for deflection are narrow, requiring individuals to meet specific requirements such as no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior. If an individual meets these requirements, officers will connect them with a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes.\n\n***\n***\n\nP.P.S. In a country where the age of addiction is getting younger, it's essential to address the issue at its root. Eating disorders and substance abuse often go hand in hand, and it's crucial to provide resources for those struggling. Shopping for groceries can be a daunting task for those struggling with addiction, and it's essential to provide support. The bitter taste of defeat can be overwhelming, but with the